    {
      "id": "l2",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "title": "Government vs corporate",
      "pages": [
        {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "Government vs corporate"
            },
            {
              "type": "image",
              "image": "obrazki/2-rzadowe-vs-korporacyjne.png",
              "width": 1,
              "maxHeight": 240,
              "caption": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "items": [
                "**Government** – issued by the state. Usually the safest, but they pay **less**",
                "**Corporate** – issued by companies. They pay **more**, because there's a higher risk the company won't pay you back"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "An issuer's creditworthiness is assessed by a **rating** (e.g. AAA = very safe)."
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "text": "Lower rating = **higher interest** and **higher risk**."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "minigame": {
        "game": "",
        "settings": []
      },
      "tasks": []
    },
    {
      "id": "l3",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "title": "Fixed vs floating rate",
      "pages": [
        {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "Fixed vs floating rate"
            },
            {
              "type": "image",
              "image": "obrazki/3-stale-vs-zmienne-oprocentowa.png",
              "width": 1,
              "maxHeight": 240,
              "caption": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "items": [
                "**Fixed coupon** – the same interest the whole time. When rates rise, the bond's price falls",
                "**Floating coupon** – interest follows interest rates or inflation, so the price is more stable"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "text": "Rates rising → **floating** is better. Rates falling → **fixed** is better."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "minigame": {
        "game": "",
        "settings": []
      },
      "tasks": []
    },
    {
      "id": "l4",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "title": "How bonds stabilise a portfolio",
      "pages": [
        {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "How bonds stabilise a portfolio"
            },
            {
              "type": "image",
              "image": "obrazki/4-jak-obligacje-stabilizuja-po.png",
              "width": 1,
              "maxHeight": 240,
              "caption": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Bonds, especially government ones, **swing much less** than stocks and often hold their value when the stock market panics."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Thanks to them, portfolio drops are **shallower** and it's easier to stick to the plan."
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "text": "It doesn't always work perfectly: in **2022** stocks and bonds fell together because interest rates rose sharply."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "minigame": {
        "game": "",
        "settings": []
      },
      "tasks": []
    },
